---
// How the homepage agent works (#18): architecture, guardrails, failure states and the evaluation
// method. Claims stay inside the agreed plan in #13 and the code on main: what exists is stated in the
// present tense; what isn't done yet (scored runs) is labelled as such. Numbers appear only once a
// committed eval run supplies them.
// Whether the agent is on is not written into the copy: the "Where it stands" aside follows the same switch
// the Worker and the homepage island follow (src/lib/agent/enabled.ts), so it can't say "off" while the agent
// is on. Update the in-progress labels as the first scored runs land.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import EvalScorecard from '../components/how/EvalScorecard.astro';
import RequestPath from '../components/how/RequestPath.astro';
import { Alert, AlertDescription } from '../components/ui/alert';
import { agentEnabled } from '../lib/agent/enabled';

const on = agentEnabled();
const repo = 'https://github.com/mitchellknoth/mitchellknoth.github.io';

const failureStates = [
  { state: 'Switched off', shows: 'The agent says it’s off and points to the work. One setting, AGENT_ENABLED, switches the Worker, and the homepage reads the same setting when the site is built. While it’s off, the homepage makes no request to the agent and shows no human check.' },
  { state: 'Budget spent', shows: 'The agent says it’s paused and, when it knows, the time it resumes.' },
  { state: 'Conversation limit', shows: 'The conversation ends and the agent points to the work.' },
  { state: 'Blocked', shows: 'The agent is unavailable from that address until its entry expires. Every page still loads.' },
  { state: 'Not verified', shows: 'A quiet Cloudflare check runs first and asks for a click only if it must. If it can’t confirm a person is asking, the agent says so.' },
  { state: 'Too fast', shows: 'A burst of requests meets Cloudflare’s rate limit. The question goes back in the field and the agent says to wait a few seconds.' },
  { state: 'Declined', shows: 'Questions outside the published work, about personal details, or trying to steer it elsewhere get a short refusal.' },
  { state: 'No answer', shows: 'If the model or the run fails, or a question needs more steps than one answer allows, it says it couldn’t answer instead of guessing.' },
  { state: 'Too long', shows: 'The question goes back in the field with the length limit.' },
];

const questionKinds = [
  { kind: 'Answerable', test: 'The published pages answer it, and the reply must cite them.' },
  { kind: 'Unanswerable', test: 'Nothing published answers it, so the agent must say so instead of guessing.' },
  { kind: 'Personal or social engineering', test: 'Declined, even when the fact is on the site.' },
  { kind: 'Numeric traps', test: 'Asks for, or assumes, a number. Only numbers in a cited source may appear.' },
  { kind: 'Prompt injection', test: 'Tries to override its instructions, add tools, reach outside the corpus or reveal the prompt.' },
];
---

<Base
  title="How the agent works"
  description="How the agent on mitchellknoth.com is built: the request path, what it can reach, its guardrails, how it fails, and how its answers are evaluated."
>
  <article class="article-layout content mx-auto how-page">
    <header class="how-header">
      <h1 class="prose-serif">How the agent on this site works</h1>
      <p class="how-lede">
        I’m building an agent for the homepage that answers questions about my work from what I’ve published here,
        and that says so and points you to the pages when it can’t help. This page shows how it’s put together, what
        limits it, how I’ll check its answers, and which parts are done.
      </p>
    </header>

    <Alert role="complementary" className="how-status" aria-label="Where it stands">
      <AlertDescription>
        <p><strong>Where it stands.</strong>
        {
          on
            ? ' The agent is switched on in production. It went live on 10 November 2026, before any scoring, so I could measure how long real answers take. On the homepage, Cloudflare’s human check runs first and stays out of the way unless it needs a click, and then a question box appears.'
            : ' The agent is switched off in production. The homepage shows a notice where the agent would appear and makes no request to it.'
        }
        This site runs on Cloudflare Workers. Built so far are the Worker, the guard, per-visitor sessions, the two
        corpus tools, refusals, the intent check, the evaluation harness and every homepage state, the last tested
        against simulated responses. I have also run the agent on the live Worker behind the human check and the
        rate limit: answers that cite the pages they used, the spending cap pausing it and the pause lifting by
        itself, a session being deleted on schedule, and the off switch. The question set is written and approved,
        but I haven’t scored the agent against it, so nothing has been scored.
        {
          on
            ? ' Until the evaluation runs are published below and I’ve reviewed the answers, read each answer with the pages it cites open. The Worker won’t show an answer that cites no page from the corpus, but whether a cited page supports the sentence is the part I haven’t checked yet.'
            : ''
        }
        It hasn’t been load tested, so I make no claims about scale.</p>
      </AlertDescription>
    </Alert>

    <h2 id="request-path">The request path</h2>
    <p>
      Every page on this site is pre-rendered and served as a file before any code runs, so the pages work the same
      whether the agent is on, off or failing. The site is served by Cloudflare Workers, and only requests to
      <code>/agent/</code> reach the Worker’s code, which runs on the same origin as the pages.
    </p>

    <RequestPath />

    <p>
      Every question passes one shared guard before it reaches a session. Each visitor gets a session of their own:
      the server picks its name and keeps it in a cookie that page scripts can’t read. The session runs the agent on
      the Pi harness, stores its conversation itself, and deletes it 24 hours after the first question. Every model call goes through AI Gateway, which holds the
      spend limit. I tested the limit on the live Worker: it paused the agent, and the pause lifted at midnight UTC.
    </p>

    <h2 id="what-it-can-reach">What it can reach</h2>
    <p>
      The agent’s only source is a corpus built from this site’s case studies, essays and résumé each time the
      site is built. The corpus changes when the site does, through a code
      change and a rebuild; nothing writes to it while the site is running.
    </p>
    <p>Two tools read it, and both only read:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><code>search_corpus</code> finds passages and returns each one with the page it came from.</li>
      <li><code>read_source</code> opens one source, chosen from the IDs fixed when the site was built.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Neither tool takes a URL. The Worker makes no outbound <code>fetch()</code> calls: its only connections are the
      platform bindings for storage, the model and the site’s own files. A build test fails if the Worker gains
      another binding, an outbound call or another tool.
    </p>

    <h2 id="guardrails">Guardrails</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Same origin only.</strong> Starting a conversation or asking a question from another site’s page is rejected.</li>
      <li><strong>Limits per conversation.</strong> A conversation has a message limit, and a question has a length limit.</li>
      <li><strong>A daily ceiling.</strong> Agent requests across all visitors are counted, and the count resets at midnight UTC.</li>
      <li><strong>A spending cap.</strong> When AI Gateway’s spend limit stops a call, the guard pauses the agent until midnight UTC, and the homepage says so.</li>
      <li><strong>An expiring blocklist.</strong> The guard refuses an address on its blocklist until the entry expires, so a shared or VPN address recovers. Only the agent is refused; nothing adds entries automatically yet.</li>
      <li><strong>An intent check.</strong> Before a question reaches the agent, a small Cloudflare model that can only return a probability, never text, checks whether it tries to steer the agent or asks for personal details. If it does, the question gets a short refusal. If the check fails or times out, the question carries on to the agent, whose own rules still apply.</li>
      <li><strong>Human checks and a rate limit.</strong> A Cloudflare challenge rule on <code>/agent/</code>, cleared by Turnstile in your browser, confirms a person is asking, and a per-address rate limit slows bursts. The check is invisible unless Cloudflare needs you to act.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="how-it-fails">How it fails</h2>
    <p>
      An agent is judged as much by how it fails as by how it answers. Each failure already has its own state in
      the homepage island, tested against simulated responses, and none of them is a dead end:
    </p>
    <dl class="fact-grid how-states">
      {
        failureStates.map(({ state, shows }) => (
          <div>
            <dt class="skills-cat">{state}</dt>
            <dd class="skills-list">{shows}</dd>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </dl>
    <p>
      Without JavaScript, the homepage is the static introduction and its links, which is where the agent would point
      you anyway.
    </p>

    <h2 id="evaluation">How I check its answers</h2>
    <p>
      I’ll score the agent against a fixed question set that I reviewed and approved before any scoring. The harness
      that runs and grades it is built and the questions are written; I haven’t run them against the agent yet. The
      set covers five kinds of question:
    </p>
    <dl class="fact-grid how-kinds">
      {
        questionKinds.map(({ kind, test }) => (
          <div>
            <dt class="skills-cat">{kind}</dt>
            <dd class="skills-list">{test}</dd>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </dl>
    <p>Grading is deterministic. An answer passes only when it meets every check that applies to it:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>It cites the sources the question expects, and every source it cites exists in the corpus.</li>
      <li>Every number in it appears in a source it cites.</li>
      <li>It refuses unanswerable and personal questions, with the expected kind of refusal.</li>
      <li>An injection attempt doesn’t change what it says, and it links to no other site.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      There’s no fluency score. If I add one, it will be reported separately and won’t change whether an answer
      passes. Each run puts the whole set to one model, against a copy of the agent on my machine and through a
      separate AI Gateway, so it never touches the visitors’ budget; that gateway gets its own spend limit before the
      first run. Each run’s scorecard is committed to the
      repository with its date. I chose the agent’s model before any scoring, and the scorecards can change that choice.
    </p>

    <EvalScorecard />

    <h2 id="source">Read the source</h2>
    <p>
      Everything on this page is in the
      <a href={repo}>site’s public repository</a>: the <a href={`${repo}/tree/main/worker`}>Worker and its guard</a>,
      the <a href={`${repo}/tree/main/src/components/agent`}>homepage island</a> and the
      <a href={`${repo}/tree/main/evals`}>evaluation set</a>.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .how-header h1 {
    margin: 0 0 1.25rem;
    color: var(--fg);
    font-size: clamp(2.5rem, 4vw, 3.5rem);
    line-height: 1.08;
  }
  .how-page .how-lede {
    max-width: 58ch;
    color: var(--fg-muted);
    font-size: 1.125rem;
  }
  /* The Alert is a React component, so Astro's scope attribute is not on it: these two are global. */
  :global(.how-page .how-status) {
    max-width: 68ch;
  }
  :global(.how-page .how-status strong) {
    color: var(--fg);
  }
  /* Inside the reading measure, two columns is the most the labels and sentences can take. */
  .how-page .fact-grid {
    max-width: 100%;
  }
  @media (min-width: 1200px) {
    .how-page .fact-grid {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    }
  }
  .how-page :global(.eval-scorecard) {
    margin-top: 3.5rem;
  }
  .how-page :global(.eval-scorecard h2) {
    margin-top: 0;
  }
  .how-page :global(.eval-scorecard p) {
    max-width: 68ch;
    padding: 1rem 1.25rem;
    border-left: 2px solid var(--editorial-border);
    color: var(--fg-muted);
  }
</style>
